(5716397, 830434, 52336, '<td valign="top">Very nice.  I like the centre-framing of the kids'' faces, and the evenness of the skin tones.  Lovely colour as well (though her bright girl-top leads my eye away from the rest of it, making me wish for something as neutral as the boy''s, but it does add a nice colour punch).  7</td>', 'Very nice.  I like the centre-framing of the kids'' faces, and the evenness of the skin tones.  Lovely colour as well (though her bright girl-top leads my eye away from the rest of it, making me wish for something as neutral as the boy''s, but it does add a nice colour punch).  7', '2009-11-06 13:35:11.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716398, 830418, 52336, '<td valign="top">I''m very interested in throw-back portraits like this.  This is nicely crafted, though the shadows are a bit too harsh in my view.  6</td>', 'I''m very interested in throw-back portraits like this.  This is nicely crafted, though the shadows are a bit too harsh in my view.  6', '2009-11-06 13:36:39.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716401, 830570, 52336, '<td valign="top">Beau cadrage, et un regard intéressant sur leur monde. 7</td>', 'Beau cadrage, et un regard intéressant sur leur monde. 7', '2009-11-06 13:40:26.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716404, 829835, 52336, '<td valign="top">Neat idea, and nice soft light for this, but too smooth in my view... would have benefited more from a restrained "film grain" effect.  Though I''m not crazy about the large area of upper right negative space, I''m interested in what''s on the backdrop.  If it''s light, bravo!  More of that would have been fantastic.  Forties-era glamour portraits usually have very idiosyncratic light arrangements on backdrops, and this reminds me of that.  (If it''s just pattern on canvas, well composed anyway.) 6<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-11-08 12:18:12.</i></td>', 'Neat idea, and nice soft light for this, but too smooth in my view... would have benefited more from a restrained "film grain" effect.  Though I''m not crazy about the large area of upper right negative space, I''m interested in what''s on the backdrop.  If it''s light, bravo!  More of that would have been fantastic.  Forties-era glamour portraits usually have very idiosyncratic light arrangements on backdrops, and this reminds me of that.  (If it''s just pattern on canvas, well composed anyway.) 6', '2009-11-06 13:46:06.000000', '2009-11-08 12:18:12.000000', 1),
(5716405, 830640, 52336, '<td valign="top">Really lovely portrait.  The best portraits are like this in my opinion, almost candids.  Nice tones.  Well done.  9</td>', 'Really lovely portrait.  The best portraits are like this in my opinion, almost candids.  Nice tones.  Well done.  9', '2009-11-06 13:47:08.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716406, 830285, 52336, '<td valign="top">The framing is good, nice and unique.  Not a lot of portraits are successful when it''s a tight shot filling more than 80% of the frame, but this one is, probably because of the way it''s framed, and the look on your face ("I don''t take myself too seriously").  The light is great, almost "Rembrandt" lighting, nice and soft.  I usually prefer well-defined catchlights in the eyes -- I can see your softbox or whatever it is, and I''d prefer to see just very bright, well-defined catchlights.  There''s a bit of detail degradation as a result of smoothing or something, on your neck and the right cheek, and that''s a bit of an issue.  Other than that, a great portrait.  Nicely done. 8</td>', 'The framing is good, nice and unique.  Not a lot of portraits are successful when it''s a tight shot filling more than 80% of the frame, but this one is, probably because of the way it''s framed, and the look on your face ("I don''t take myself too seriously").  The light is great, almost "Rembrandt" lighting, nice and soft.  I usually prefer well-defined catchlights in the eyes -- I can see your softbox or whatever it is, and I''d prefer to see just very bright, well-defined catchlights.  There''s a bit of detail degradation as a result of smoothing or something, on your neck and the right cheek, and that''s a bit of an issue.  Other than that, a great portrait.  Nicely done. 8', '2009-11-06 13:47:48.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716407, 830427, 43774, '<td valign="top">Lovely photo, looks good in B&amp;W, would also like to see the colour version. 8 from me.</td>', 'Lovely photo, looks good in B&W, would also like to see the colour version. 8 from me.', '2009-11-06 13:48:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716408, 830584, 52336, '<td valign="top">Technically wonderful and professionally executed, and a unique and perfectly done duotone, but nothing for me to connect with.  7 for the technicals.</td>', 'Technically wonderful and professionally executed, and a unique and perfectly done duotone, but nothing for me to connect with.  7 for the technicals.', '2009-11-06 13:48:21.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716410, 829213, 52336, '<td valign="top">Quite an interesting and different light setup for a kid (not one I''ve often seen).  Something like this is probably considered a no-no by people better than me, as it makes the kid look kind of menacing.  What do I know, I like it.  What with the decision to light one side and edge light the other, and the look on his face, this otherwise straightforward portrait takes on a different, darker meaning.  Nicely put together.  Other than that, and it''s pleasing composition, I''m not fond of what was done in post to make it look so artificially smooth, or perhaps sharpened+denoised, or something.  7</td>', 'Quite an interesting and different light setup for a kid (not one I''ve often seen).  Something like this is probably considered a no-no by people better than me, as it makes the kid look kind of menacing.  What do I know, I like it.  What with the decision to light one side and edge light the other, and the look on his face, this otherwise straightforward portrait takes on a different, darker meaning.  Nicely put together.  Other than that, and it''s pleasing composition, I''m not fond of what was done in post to make it look so artificially smooth, or perhaps sharpened+denoised, or something.  7', '2009-11-06 13:48:56.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716411, 825722, 52336, '<td valign="top">Wow, great example of an oft-photographed scene.  Nicely processed. 7</td>', 'Wow, great example of an oft-photographed scene.  Nicely processed. 7', '2009-11-06 13:49:04.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716413, 830224, 52336, '<td valign="top">This is a fine example of such a photograph, but I really must say that I''ve seen enough of these.  It''s a tricky capture, and I don''t have the patience or talent for this, but I never connect with them; they leave me flat.  6 for the technicals.</td>', 'This is a fine example of such a photograph, but I really must say that I''ve seen enough of these.  It''s a tricky capture, and I don''t have the patience or talent for this, but I never connect with them; they leave me flat.  6 for the technicals.', '2009-11-06 13:51:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716417, 827466, 52336, '<td valign="top">Much too washed out, as though over-corrected in post from a photograph that was probably not set up well as far as lighting goes to begin with.  Composition for this subject with the feeling you are trying to convey is odd, probably a mistake.  The difference between the foreground and background is very contrasty, a bit too much.  4, I''m afraid. :(</td>', 'Much too washed out, as though over-corrected in post from a photograph that was probably not set up well as far as lighting goes to begin with.  Composition for this subject with the feeling you are trying to convey is odd, probably a mistake.  The difference between the foreground and background is very contrasty, a bit too much.  4, I''m afraid. :(', '2009-11-06 13:54:13.000000', NULL, 1),
(5716425, 829186, 52336, '<td valign="top">An interesting capture, but a word on the title: I often come across titles like this, and they are an anomaly.  Waiting for what?  If you are interested in appropriately titling your work, try comparing titles of successful pros.  You''ll come across titles like "Flowers", "June bugs", "Pretty dress", "On the porch", and so on.  Very simple, straightforward monikers that let the photograph do the talking, not the title.  A title like yours in my view tries to make the viewer think a certain way about a picture, and I''m not sure that''s a primary goal for us.  It might have been better titled something like "Church Pew", or "Afternoon Light".  Allow your title to describe what your photo is, not what we should be thinking while viewing it.  Just a thought, and I''ve used oblique titles myself very often, but the importance of the title has concerned me for a while now. 5</td>', 'An interesting capture, but a word on the title: I often come across titles like this, and they are an anomaly.  Waiting for what?  If you are interested in appropriately titling your work, try comparing titles of successful pros.  You''ll come across titles like "Flowers", "June bugs", "Pretty dress", "On the porch", and so on.  Very simple, straightforward monikers that let the photograph do the talking, not the title.  A title like yours in my view tries to make the viewer think a certain way about a picture, and I''m not sure that''s a primary goal for us.  It might have been better titled something like "Church Pew", or "Afternoon Light".  Allow your title to describe what your photo is, not what we should be thinking while viewing it.  Just a thought, and I''ve used oblique titles myself very often, but the importance of the title has concerned me for a while now. 5', '2009-11-06 14:03:59.000000', NULL, 1),
